WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.079 --> 00:00:01.576
- [Excited Man] Carolina Reaper!

00:00:01.576 --> 00:00:03.074
- [Camera Man] Go ahead
and put it in your mouth.

00:00:03.074 --> 00:00:04.368
- Go!

00:00:04.368 --> 00:00:06.121
- [Narrator] Since the
first time it was announced

00:00:06.121 --> 00:00:08.617
to the public that we were
growing these, there were

00:00:08.617 --> 00:00:12.704
literally thousands of YouTube
videos of people eating

00:00:12.704 --> 00:00:16.512
Carolina Reapers all over the world.

00:00:16.512 --> 00:00:18.381
I get a kick out of most of them.

00:00:18.381 --> 00:00:20.599
- I made a huge mistake!

00:00:20.599 --> 00:00:23.722
- Though it can be unpleasant,
it's not a dangerous thing

00:00:23.722 --> 00:00:25.638
to eat super hot peppers.

00:00:25.638 --> 00:00:26.845
- Oh my God!

00:00:26.845 --> 00:00:28.471
It's only getting worse!

00:00:28.471 --> 00:00:29.396
(coughing)

00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:31.642
- [Narrator] It's a chemical
reaction your brain perceives

00:00:31.642 --> 00:00:34.440
as heat so you will stop doing it.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.648
- I'm gonna lay down.

00:00:35.648 --> 00:00:37.355
This sucks.

00:00:37.355 --> 00:00:41.438
(uptempo modern classical music)

00:00:47.908 --> 00:00:49.742
- I'm “Smokin’” Ed Currie.

00:00:49.742 --> 00:00:52.831
I created the Carolina Reaper.

00:00:52.831 --> 00:00:56.226
I got into breeding peppers
in college and just really

00:00:56.226 --> 00:00:59.302
was interested in getting
hotter and hotter peppers

00:00:59.302 --> 00:01:01.346
once I tried them.

00:01:01.346 --> 00:01:03.679
Guinness awarded me the
world record for the hottest

00:01:03.679 --> 00:01:05.929
pepper in November of 2013.

00:01:07.104 --> 00:01:09.519
To breed these things,
I crossed a ghost pepper

00:01:09.519 --> 00:01:12.561
with a habanero, and what
came out was this evil

00:01:12.561 --> 00:01:15.231
looking pepper that had warts all over it,

00:01:15.231 --> 00:01:17.495
blisters they call them.

00:01:17.495 --> 00:01:21.662
And the first time I tasted
it, it knocked me to my knees.

00:01:23.619 --> 00:01:25.360
(uptempo flamenco guitar)

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.205
We get our peppers tested
regularly at Winthrop University

00:01:28.205 --> 00:01:30.875
by doctor Calloway in the chemistry lab.

00:01:30.875 --> 00:01:33.917
All peppers are measured
by Scoville heat units,

00:01:33.917 --> 00:01:36.436
which tell you how hot the pepper is.

00:01:36.436 --> 00:01:39.467
Bell peppers have zero
Scoville heat units.

00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:43.553
Jalapeño is about 5,000, a Serrano 20,000,

00:01:43.553 --> 00:01:47.803
red habaneros 200,000,
ghost peppers 600,000,

00:01:47.803 --> 00:01:50.101
then you get to the Carolina Reaper,

00:01:50.101 --> 00:01:53.271
which is 1,569,000 heat
units, the hottest thing

00:01:53.271 --> 00:01:54.899
in the world.

00:01:54.899 --> 00:01:55.978
(playful string music)

00:01:55.978 --> 00:01:58.312
When you eat the Carolina
Reaper, the first sensation

00:01:58.312 --> 00:02:01.029
you get is sweet, and then
all of a sudden the heat

00:02:01.029 --> 00:02:04.070
starts going, it's like
burning, burning, burning!

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:06.462
Then the physiological stuff starts.

00:02:06.462 --> 00:02:08.796
Your eyes will bloodshot, they just start

00:02:08.796 --> 00:02:11.710
tearing up and watering, the skin flushes,

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:14.171
you start breathing hard, your metabolism

00:02:14.171 --> 00:02:15.506
starts raising up.

00:02:15.506 --> 00:02:18.502
Some people say it's
like eating molten lava.

00:02:18.502 --> 00:02:21.044
Others will say it's like licking the sun.

00:02:21.044 --> 00:02:23.413
To me, it's like eating
a charcoal briquette

00:02:23.413 --> 00:02:25.909
and no matter what you do
you cannot get that burn

00:02:25.909 --> 00:02:27.345
out of the back of your mouth.

00:02:27.345 --> 00:02:29.342
I mean it's crazy.

00:02:29.342 --> 00:02:31.943
A lot of what I do is creative expression.

00:02:31.943 --> 00:02:34.137
You look for traits in
one pepper, and you look

00:02:34.137 --> 00:02:36.355
for traits in another and
you try to combine them

00:02:36.355 --> 00:02:39.362
and keep both those traits.

00:02:39.362 --> 00:02:41.904
There are other breeders who
are trying to break our record,

00:02:41.904 --> 00:02:45.747
but, uh, we've been holding unreleased
peppers in our back pocket

00:02:45.747 --> 00:02:48.661
that are twice as hot or
more than the Carolina Reaper

00:02:48.661 --> 00:02:52.075
that we could submit to
Guinness at any time.

00:02:52.075 --> 00:02:54.780
My perception of what
the limits are of how hot

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.415
you can breed a pepper
has changed as the science

00:02:57.415 --> 00:02:59.668
has progressed over the years.

00:02:59.668 --> 00:03:01.548
Just tell me something can't
be done, I'm gonna figure

00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:03.131
out a way to do it.

